{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:historian","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:49fc4fd38791d36124407c76a2500d3bdc0695c2c60eff3049ed9d218e48059e","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.17,"B":0.53,"C":0.13,"D":0.17}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Applied the lexical priority lock, first match wins, using my own CQ3 (P10 1.05 / P50 2.00 / P90 2.95; modelled as left-skewed with implied sigma ~0.74) and my own OECD-38 GDP-weighted core inflation path for 2032-36 (central 2.1-2.4%, from the explicit basket-weight arithmetic in my CQ2 reasoning).\nSTEP 1 - A (growth >=2.6%). Normal approximation on my CQ3 gives P = 1-Phi(0.6/0.74) = 21%; trimmed to 17% for the left skew (demographic denominators are near-deterministic, so the right tail is thinner than Gaussian). Outside-view tension noted and deliberately not resolved to zero: 11 of 13 historical 5-yr windows since 1960 exceeded 2.6%, so A is the HISTORICALLY NORMAL outcome and only the weight/labour-force arithmetic argues it away. Mechanically, A needs OECD ~1.9% (per-capita ~1.7% vs the locked 0.6% baseline, a +1.1pp offset) with China ~4% - beyond AI-Q1's >=+0.50pp at 23%. A = 17.\nSTEP 2 - C (core >=2.7% AND growth <2.0%). P(OECD-38 GDP-weighted core 2032-36 >=2.7%) = 0.20 from my CQ2 decomposition (a 5-yr average has more variance than the 10-yr average that drove my 0.27 at the 2.50 bar, but 2.7 is a higher bar and 2032-36 is the back half, where my brief's disinflationary forces - capex digestion, excess capacity, China export deflation, the BIS Box C demand drag - dominate). P(growth<2.0) = 0.50 from my CQ3 P50. Stagflation correlation rho ~ +0.15 (supply-wall regime), so P(growth<2.0 | core>=2.7) ~ 0.58 -> 0.116. Base-rate cross-check: 5-yr windows with OECD growth <2% and core >=2.7% since 1960 number ~2.5 of 13 = 19%, but all of them predate inflation targeting. Blended with a weight on regime persistence: C = 13.\nSTEP 3 - D (growth <1.7% AND core <2.0%). P(growth<1.7) = Phi(-0.41) = 34%, ~37% with left skew. P(core<2.0 | growth<1.7) ~ 0.55 (positive correlation; this is the demand-shortfall cell) -> 0.20. HARD outside-view constraint applied: NO 5-year window of OECD+China PPP growth since 1960 has come in below 1.7% - even 2008-2012 was ~2.4% - so the unconditional base rate for D is ~0/13. D is live only because of the never-before-seen arithmetic (China at 34% weight growing under 3% while OECD labour force shrinks). I pull D from 0.20 down to 0.17 to respect the zero base rate without discarding the arithmetic. D = 17.\nSTEP 4 - B = residual = 100 - 17 - 13 - 17 = 53. Sum = 100 exactly.\nCONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED. (i) vs CQ3: my P50 of 2.00% falls squarely inside B's residual cell (growth 1.7-2.5% with core 2.0-2.6%), and A at 17% matches P(growth>=2.6) read off my own distribution rather than being set independently. (ii) vs CQ2: I put 14% on OECD core >=2.50% over 2027-36 jointly with China <1.50%. C requires a HIGHER inflation bar (>=2.7%) over a SHORTER, LATER window and does not require the China conjunct; dropping the China conjunct raises it, the higher bar and later window lower it, and 13% sits consistently alongside 14%. (iii) The relative-price skew central to my effort - electricity, construction and care at 4-6% while software, media, professional services and tradable goods run 0-1% - is definitionally the B cell: it produces an unremarkable CORE aggregate precisely because the hot items are excluded from core (household energy), weight-light, or publicly administered. That is why B carries the majority of my mass while C stays low despite extreme dispersion in the underlying prices.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:56.426Z","seriesId":"publishe